# rule.require.generic-governs-structure-never-voice

> **telepath governs the STRUCTURE of a message. it governs the VOICE not at all.**

| axis | who owns it | examples |
|---|---|---|
| **structure** | 🔮 telepath | what the message says, in what order, in how many words, where each passage sits |
| **voice** | the adopter's mascot + the role that speaks | capitalization, emoji, persona, vibe phrases, mascot lines |

the two **compose**. a telepath rule fires inside an owl's voice here, a seaturtle's in `ehmpathy`,
and any voice a future repo adopts — unchanged.

## .why — a generic that carries a voice cannot BE generic

**a structural rule is repo-agnostic by nature.** *"lead with the point"*, *"a passage sits where
its subject sits"*, *"answer what they meant"* hold for an owl, a seaturtle, a beaver, and a
corporate style guide alike. so the rule is written once and adopted everywhere.

**a voice rule is repo-specific by nature.** 🦉 belongs to this repo and 🐢 to `ehmpathy`; lowercase
is a house style, not a transfer property. the moment a generic carries one, a repo that wants the
structure must take the foreign voice with it or fork the rule — and a forked rule drifts on the
structural half, which is the half that was worth a shared home.

⇒ **the seam is what makes the role reusable.** cross it and telepath becomes a second persona
brief, which every repo already has.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| you reach for an emoji in a telepath prescription | that is voice. state the structural property; let the adopter's mascot supply the glyph |
| you write *"say it in lowercase"* or *"open with the mascot"* | voice — it belongs in `im_a.<mascot>` |
| you write *"lead with the point, then the detail"* | structure — it is telepath's |
| an example needs a concrete message to show the shape | use this repo's voice — an example illustrates, it does not prescribe |
| a repo adopts a rule and its output looks wrong | check which axis broke. wrong order = telepath's defect. wrong glyph = the mascot's |

## .the test

> **would this rule read the same if the reader's mascot were a seaturtle instead of an owl?**

yes → structure. it is telepath's · no → voice. it belongs to whichever repo adopts it

## .examples — one rule, written three ways

| the specimen | the prescription | what an adopter can do with it |
|---|---|---|
| 👍 the bare rule | *"lead with the answer; the reason follows it"* | an owl obeys in lowercase with a 🦉, a seaturtle with a 🐢. the rule did not say, so both adopt it unchanged |
| 👎 the leaked rule | *"lead with the answer, in lowercase, prefixed with 🦉"* | `ehmpathy` cannot take it and keep its own mascot, so it forks — and the two copies drift on the structural half |
| 👍 the demoed rule | *"an error names the fix, not merely the symptom"*, demoed as `🦉 bummer — no route bound. bind one with: rhx route.bind.set --route <path>` | the rule wears no voice and travels; only its example does |

⇒ **the demoed rule is the one to hold.** the bare and the leaked part a good rule from a bad one;
the demoed one shows that a concrete example is not a leak — an example illustrates, and only a
prescription binds.

## 🟡 .the owed migration

> **the output-half generics are not here yet. their move is owed and not yet done.**

they live in `ehmpathy`'s mechanic + architect roles under `lang.prose/` — beside `lang.tones/`,
that repo's voice cluster.

⇒ that co-location is this seam, drawn independently by another repo. when the move happens,
`lang.prose/` comes and `lang.tones/` stays.

## 🟡 .the voice row is declared in ONE cell — cite it, never copy it

a peer rule that restates the members will drift from them, and the drift is silent: no reader ever
holds two copies at once, so a copy that is short one member reads as complete.

⇒ **so every rule in this canon points at the table above rather than re-lists it.**

measured 2026-09-07, across telepath's briefs: **18** source files restated the four members in
`.the axis`, and **2** had already drifted — `forbid.absolutes` wrote `glyph` where the row says
`emoji`; `forbid.emphasis-noise` wrote `glyph, case, or persona`, two members renamed and
`mascot line` dropped, so a copy short one member read as complete. no `.md.min` restated it.

⇒ **each copy was authored by someone who had just read the row, and two drifted anyway.** a copy
must be re-checked on every edit; a reference cannot go stale.

blocker: a telepath rule that prescribes a member of the **voice** row — it makes the rule
unadoptable · a mascot brief that prescribes message order, length, or passage placement — it is
structure, and it will drift from the generic that owns it · a peer rule that restates the voice row
rather than cites it.
false positive: a telepath example written in the adopter's voice — an example must be concrete to
illustrate at all.
